Z||000000||ezsurf||07-22-99||07:46 PM||||<br>I believe the shift knob just screws on to the shift lever, but someone tell me if this is so.  Right now I don't have 3rd or 4th gear and I need to do the adjustment procedure on the shift cables.  I can't get the console out because I can't get the shift knob off.  Someone has probably gorilla torqued it so I can double my pleasure and double my fun. 
Z||000001||PaUL||07-22-99||07:48 PM||||yeah the knob is supposed just screw off, at least mine does. Yours may have been glued on. Maybe a previous owner used locktite on the threads.
Z||000002||Punkndrublic||07-23-99||12:03 AM||||Mine is screwed on too and comes off really easily.<p>If your shifter hasn't been shortened yet, why not get one, or make one then hack the old one off and replace it? but then i suppose you won't be able to double your pleasure and double your fun.
Z||000003||G25C||07-23-99||09:26 AM||||Im a little curious.  I have a 87 GT.  I've seen the shorter stick for sale and was wondering why someone would replace there original with the shorter one.  Does it make shifting easier and more exact?  Ive noticed that sometimes when shifting my Fiero its kinda ackward, but it always goes into gear.<p>just wondering...
Z||000004||Taijiguy||07-31-99||02:38 PM||||I have an 85 GT I built up, and along the way found myself with a hacksaw in my hand, eyeballing the shifter. 2 - 3 inches later, and 15 bucks into a die, and another 15 bucks into a Hurst "T" handle, and I had a shortened shifter. It's great. The original shifter was high and uncomfortable, the new height is much more comfortable, plus it does shift faster..meaning shorter throw...but takes some getting used to. A modification like this would have solved Racer 1's problem with  his parents wanting to drive his Fiero! It's an enthusiast's mod for sure.
